# Shell Credentials — Always Source Profile First

When executing shell commands that require API credentials (Jira, Snowflake, Salesforce, GitHub, etc.), **always source `~/.zshrc` first** before using environment variables.

## Rule

Before any command that references `$JIRA_EMAIL`, `$JIRA_TOKEN`, `$JIRA_BASE_URL`, `$SNOWFLAKE_*`, or any other credential environment variable:

1. Run `source ~/.zshrc 2>/dev/null` at the start of the command chain.
2. If credentials are still empty after sourcing, also check `~/.profile` and `~/.zprofile`.
3. Never ask the user to re-export credentials that are already in their shell profile.

## Known Credential Locations in `~/.zshrc`

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `JIRA_EMAIL` | Jira API username |
| `JIRA_TOKEN` | Jira API token |
| `JIRA_BASE_URL` | Jira instance URL (e.g., `https://workdaybt.atlassian.net`) |
| `JIRA_PROJECT` | Default Jira project key |

## Example

```bash
# CORRECT — always source first
source ~/.zshrc 2>/dev/null; curl -s -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_TOKEN" "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/2/issue/TICKET-123"

# WRONG — credentials will be empty in a new shell session
curl -s -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_TOKEN" ...
```

## Jira API Notes

- Jira REST API may return JSON with invalid escape sequences (e.g., `\!`, `\S`). Always sanitize with `re.sub(r'(?<!\\)\\(?!["\\/bfnrtu])', r'\\\\', raw)` before `json.loads()`.
- Use `curl` for Jira API calls (not Python `urllib`) to avoid SSL certificate issues in sandboxed environments.
